PROTOTYPE ONLY (ADR 0027 proposal, new windows on the Linux host,
docs/measurements/2026-11-20-new-windows-ubuntu.md). Never include this patch in a build
used for a trial, the gate or a verdict.

Apply to gst-wayland-display (MIT License), commit 1301f86. Apply the four measured patches
in scripts/patches first, in order: render-on-commit, cursor-messages, windows, dialogs.
Then apply scripts/patches/proto/gst-wayland-display-proto-second-output.patch (U0),
followed by this patch, scripts/patches/proto/gst-wayland-display-proto-second-output-on-commit.patch.
The context comes from that project and those patches.

The element with output=1 can now set render-on-commit for its own output. A commit of its
window releases a waiting frame as soon as the frame rate allows. After two quiet frame
intervals a frame goes out to keep the stream running. Turning the switch off restores the
U0 beat. As in the measured patches for the first output, the second output renders the
frame, copies it into the element's buffer, then waits for it. This patch keeps that order.
Frame requests, rate limits and draw callbacks belong to each output separately.
The second window gets presentation feedback from the output that rendered it. The tests
use two software outputs and a Wayland client.

This step covers pacing only. Input for the second window, popups, dialogs, resizing,
window lists and changes to the transport remain outside the prototype patch.

